# A repo should declare its own keur policy so the gate is explicit and shared,
# not left to per-machine defaults. file-absence over the repo scope (ADR-052):
# fires when neither <repo>/.config/keur.toml nor <repo>/keur.toml exists.
# Basename match, so either location counts.
#
# Two-rule escalation (ADR-026: maturity decides IF active, action decides how
# hard): this warn is active from maturity 2 (developing). KEUR-CFG-002 blocks
# the same gap from maturity 4 (managed). A low-maturity repo gets a nudge; a
# mature one must commit a policy. The overlap between the two is the kind of
# "same issue, two rules" ADR-053 (clustering) will fold in the report.
id: KEUR-CFG-001
ilities: [analysability, modifiability]
maturity: 2
title: Repository has no keur config (.config/keur.toml or keur.toml)
category: quality
severity: warning
engine: file-absence
action: warn
target:
  filenames: keur.toml
confidence: 0.6
message: "No keur config at repo level — add .config/keur.toml (preferred, keeps the root clean) or keur.toml so the gate's policy is explicit, shared and reviewable, not left to per-machine defaults."
fix: "Create .config/keur.toml with at least a [difficulty] block (ADR-026) and the [policy] thresholds; commit it so the whole team runs the same gate."
